dzn: Keep emulated ASTC and ETC2 colour textures as BC3

Dozen emulated ASTC and ETC2 by decoding each upload with Mesa's compute
decoders into an RGBA8 image, four bytes a texel. Games that ship ASTC
(3DMark Wild Life Extreme's are 8x8, a quarter of a byte a texel) then read
four times the memory gfxstream's BC3 textures need, and on an integrated GPU,
which shares the host's memory, the extra bandwidth is the whole gap between
Venus and gfxstream on WSL2: a Radeon 780M scored 3,961 in Wild Life Extreme
with Venus, 4,323 with gfxstream, and 3,980 with gfxstream made to keep RGBA8
(IKA_ASTC_DECODE=cpu-rgba8).

Where D3D12 takes BC images of any size (UnalignedBlockTexturesSupported),
ASTC and the ETC2 colour formats are now BC3 images, sampled natively, a
quarter of RGBA8's memory; EAC keeps its 16-bit formats. A level is still
decoded by the same decoder, but into an RGBA8 image of the command buffer's
own, and a compute shader (dzn_bc3_encode_spv.h, with its GLSL) encodes the
texels into BC3 blocks: bounding-box endpoints along the colour covariance,
one least-squares refinement, eight-value alpha. The blocks go into the image
with CopyTextureRegion: D3D12 refuses unordered access to BC formats without
relaxed format casting, and Dozen's own copies take the block size from the
image's Vulkan format. A copy that starts or ends inside a 4x4 BC block decodes
the whole block from the level's other source blocks, which the emulated image
keeps. The scratch image, block buffer and descriptor sets live until the
command buffer is reset. Between the decode and the encode the scratch image
changes layout, and around the copy the block buffer changes state, so that
without enhanced barriers (an Iris Plus 650's driver) Dozen's legacy barriers
order them: a barrier that keeps a layout makes no D3D12 transition there.
DZN_DEBUG=nobc3emulation keeps RGBA8.

